<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jeong Do-jeon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1342">1342</a>–<a href="/years/1398">1398</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jeong Do-jeon was a Korean Neo-Confucian scholar and statesman who was the chief architect of the Joseon dynasty. He helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-joseon">Yi Seong-gye</a> seize power from Goryeo, drafted the land reform of 1391, designed the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a> and wrote the first codes of Joseon government. A fierce critic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, he sought a state led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, but was killed by the prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejong-of-joseon">Yi Bang-won</a> in 1398.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jeong-do-jeon.jpg" alt="Jeong Do-jeon" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve46814 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korea-Danyang-Dodamsambong_Sam_Bong_statue_3076-07.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chŏng Tojŏn, also known by his art name Sambong, was born on 6 October 1342 in Yeongju, in Goryeo, into the Bonghwa Chŏng clan. His family had emerged from commoner status about four generations earlier and had slowly risen through government service, and his father was the first of them to reach a high post. According to the Veritable Records his maternal grandmother was a slave, though the credibility of that account is questioned. He studied under Yi Che-hyŏn alongside other leading thinkers such as Chŏng Mong-ju. He first met Yi Sŏnggye in 1383, when he visited him at his quarters in Hamgyong province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Yi Sŏnggye, later King Taejo, founded Joseon in July 1392, he appointed Chŏng Tojŏn to the highest civilian and military office at once. Chŏng decided policy from military affairs and diplomacy to education. He laid down Joseon's political system and tax laws, replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> as the national religion, moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Hanyang</a>, present-day Seoul, and wrote a code of laws that became in effect Joseon's constitution. He chose the names of the palaces, the eight provinces and the districts of the capital, worked to free many slaves and reformed land policy.</p><p>He saw Joseon as a kingdom led by ministers, with a largely symbolic king, and held that government, including the king, existed for the sake of the people. He argued that Goryeo's rulers had given up their right to rule. He also urged that private armies, including the princes', be abolished and sent to the central government. Yi Pangwŏn, Taejo's fifth son, was displeased, and none of the princes complied. Chŏng persuaded Taejo to name his young eighth son Yi Pangsŏk crown prince instead of Yi Pangwŏn, who had done most to bring his father to power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1398, after the sudden death of Queen Sindeok, Yi Pangwŏn raided the palace in the coup known as the First Strife of the Princes. He killed Chŏng Tojŏn and his supporters and the queen's two sons, including the crown prince. The Veritable Records show Chŏng begging for his life, while the Sambong chip portrays him leaving a death poem and accepting death with dignity. Neither account is held fully credible. Taejo abdicated, and Yi Pangwŏn later became King Taejong.</p><p>For about three centuries Chŏng was regarded as a treacherous villain, and the philosopher Song Si-yŏl always called him insidious. In the 18th century King Chŏngjo republished the Sambong chip, and Chŏng was rehabilitated in 1865 in recognition of his role in designing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongbokgung">Gyeongbokgung</a>. His treatise Bulssi Japbyeon attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> as corrupt in practice and nihilistic in doctrine, as a student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>'s thought.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chŏng Tojŏn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Allied with reform-minded Neo-Confucian officials led by <b>Jeong Do-jeon</b>, he carried out a land reform in 1391, the Rank Land Law (gwajeon), which broke the estates of the old aristocracy and endowed his supporters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Danyang County, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.99,128.36&amp;year=1378" aria-label="Danyang County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7768 831 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jeong Do-jeon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8218 1101h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8218 1101h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.99,128.36&amp;year=1378">What was here in 1378? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
